{"results":[{"url":"https://libguides.csun.edu/evaluating-sources/misinformation-disinformation-and-fake-news","final_url":"https://libguides.csun.edu/evaluating-sources/misinformation-disinformation-and-fake-news","title":"LibGuides: Evaluating Sources: Misinformation, Disinformation & Fake News","description":"LibGuides: Evaluating Sources: Misinformation, Disinformation & Fake News","language":"en","text":"Skip to Main Content\n\n# Evaluating Sources: Misinformation, Disinformation & Fake News\n\n## Understanding Information Disorder\n\nNot all false or misleading information is created or shared for the same reasons. Understanding the differences between misinformation, disinformation, and malinformation can help you better evaluate information encountered online.\n\n### Misinformation\n\nFalse or inaccurate information that is shared without the intent to deceive.\n\n**Example:**  \nSharing an outdated news story believing it is current.\n\n### Disinformation\n\nFalse information that is deliberately created or shared to mislead others.\n\n**Example:**  \nCreating a fake news website to influence public opinion.\n\n### Malinformation\n\nAccurate information that is shared to cause harm.\n\n**Example:**  \nSharing private information without permission.\n\nWardle, C., & Derakhshan, H. (2017).  Council of Europe.\n\n## The Seven Types of Mis- and Disinformation\n\n### Mis-, dis-, and malinformation\n\nThe Venn diagram explains three types of information disorder. Misinformation consists of unintentional mistakes such as inaccurate photo captions, dates, statistics, translation, or when satire is taken seriously. Disinformation is fabricated or deliberately manipulated audio or visual content, including intentionally created conspiracy theories or rumors. Malinformation is the deliberate publication of private information for personal or corporate public interest, or the deliberate change of context, date, or time of genuine content. The Venn diagram shows that misinformation is primarily related to falseness, malinformation is primarily related to intent to harm, and disinformation overlaps both.\n\n### Satire or Parody\n\nNo intention to cause harm.\n\n### False Connection\n\nHeadlines, visuals, or captions do not support the content.\n\n### Misleading Content\n\nInformation presented in a deceptive way.\n\n### False Context\n\nGenuine content shared with incorrect contextual information.\n\n### Imposter Content\n\nSources impersonate legitimate organizations or people.\n\n### Manipulated Content\n\nAuthentic information or images altered to deceive.\n\n### Fabricated Content\n\nEntirely false content created to deceive.\n\n## Recognizing Fake News\n\n| Warning Sign | Why It Matters |\n| --- | --- |\n| Sensational headlines | Designed to attract attention or provoke strong emotional reactions. |\n| Emotional language | Can influence readers' emotions rather than encourage critical thinking. |\n| Lack of evidence | Reliable information should include supporting evidence or credible sources. |\n| Anonymous sources | Anonymous or unnamed sources can make credibility difficult to assess. |\n| Missing author information | Knowing who created the content helps evaluate expertise and authority. |\n| No supporting coverage | Major news is often confirmed by multiple independent, reliable sources. |\n| Manipulated images or videos | Images and videos may be edited or presented out of context to mislead audiences. |\n\n## Applying the SIFT Method\n\nCTRL-F: Digital Media Literacy\n\nWatch digital literacy expert Mike Caulfield demonstrate how to investigate online information using the same critical thinking strategies summarized in the SIFT Method below.\n\n## The SIFT Method\n\nWhen encountering information online, use the SIFT method developed by digital literacy expert Mike Caulfield to quickly evaluate claims and sources.\n\n### S: Stop\n\nPause before sharing or reacting.\n\n**Ask:**\n\n* What do I know about this source?\n* What evidence is provided?\n\n### I: Investigate the Source\n\nLearn more about the author, organization, or website.\n\n**Look for:**\n\n* Credentials\n* Reputation\n* Independent information\n\n### F: Find Better Coverage\n\nSee whether other reliable sources are reporting the same information.\n\n**Look for:**\n\n* Consensus\n* Additional context\n* Independent reporting\n\n### T: Trace Claims to the Original Context\n\nFollow quotes, images, statistics, and claims back to their original source.\n\n**Ask:**\n\n* Is this being presented accurately?\n* Has context been removed?\n\n### Before You Share\n\n**Stop → Verify → Compare → Investigate → Think Critically**\n\n## Why Do People Share False Information?\n\n### People Share False Information for Many Reasons:\n\n* They believe it is true.\n* The information supports their existing beliefs.\n* The content triggers a strong emotional response.\n* They want to entertain others.\n* They want attention or engagement.\n* They intend to mislead or manipulate.\n\nSharing misinformation does not always require malicious intent.\n\n* Last Updated: Sep 17, 2026 1:14 PM\n* URL: https://libguides.csun.edu/evaluating-sources\n* Print Page\n\nLogin to LibApps","author":null,"published_date":null,"latency_ms":415.38779900292866,"format":"markdown"}],"errors":[]}